ek to discuss my academic plan and any concerns I might have. After lunch, we had a practical session on using the university's online systems: Brightspace (the learning management system), the library databases, and various student services portals. While these systems seem well-designed, there's still a lot to navigate and remember. The afternoon included a panel discussion with current students and recent graduates of the program. Their insights were invaluable\u2014they spoke candidly about workload management, recommended study strategies, and shared their experiences with group projects and assessments. One panelist, Mei, who graduated last year and now works at Accenture's Dublin office, emphasized the importance of building a portfolio of projects alongside coursework. Another, James, advised focusing on practical implementation skills rather than just theoretical knowledge, noting that recruiters particularly value problem-solving abilities. The most sobering part of the day was a session on academic integrity and plagiarism policies. The standards here seem much stricter than what I was accustomed to in India, with sophisticated plagiarism detection software and significant penalties for violations. I'll need to be extremely careful about proper citation and referencing in all my work. The day ended with a casual mixer where I had the chance to speak with some professors one-on-one. Dr. Sharma mentioned that he sometimes takes on research assistants from the master's program, which could be an excellent opportunity to gain research experience. I made sure to express my interest in Machine Learning applications for healthcare, which seemed to pique his curiosity. On my way home, I stopped by the university bookstore to purchase some recommended textbooks. The prices were higher than I expected\u2014academic texts here cost significantly more than in India, even accounting for the exchange rate. I'll need to make greater use of the library and electronic resources to stay within my budget. This evening, I'm feeling a mix of enthusiasm and apprehension. The program promises to be intellectually stimulating but also incredibly demanding. The competitive atmosphere was palpable today, with many students clearly driven to excel. While I've always performed well academically, I wonder how I'll measure up in this international environment with different educational norms and expectations. Classes begin on Monday, and I'm determined to start strong, establish good study habits from the beginning, and make the most of every learning opportunity this program offers.", "response": "{\n  \"sentiment_score\": 0.6,\n  \"emotion_tags\": [\"excited\", \"intimidated\", \"hopeful\", \"apprehensive\", \"motivated\"]\n}", "metadata": {"assistant_id": "asst_g27M1YXi1MnsjzN4NT9neuka", "thread_id": "thread_DOPN4Wda4o3pDse9Y2kNzCfO", "used_tools": false}}
