ally be an advantage, offering connections outside the academic bubble and a quieter study environment than typical student housing. I told them I needed to think it over but was definitely interested. As I left, Lisa mentioned they were hoping to decide by tomorrow evening, so I don't have much time to consider or look at other options. On my way back to my temporary accommodation, I called my parents to discuss the housing situation. They agreed that while the Rathmines option is expensive, having a comfortable and friendly living environment is worth the investment, especially given the academic pressures ahead. They also reminded me that we had budgeted for housing costs at approximately this level. With their support, I've decided to accept the Rathmines room and have emailed Aoife to confirm my interest. If all goes well, I can move in next weekend, which would give me stability before classes intensify. The rest of my day was spent preparing for the academic challenges ahead. I reviewed some fundamental concepts in programming and mathematics that will be relevant for my courses. I'm particularly concerned about the Machine Learning module, as it requires strong statistical knowledge. While I covered these topics in my undergraduate studies, I want to ensure my foundation is solid before building upon it. In the evening, I joined a video call with the Indian Society's committee members. They're organizing a welcome event for new Indian students next Friday, and I've volunteered to help with arrangements. The society seems active, with regular cultural celebrations, professional networking events, and social gatherings. It will be nice to connect with the Indian community here, though I'm equally committed to integrating with the broader international student body. Before bed, I exchanged messages with Akash, my closest friend from undergraduate days who's doing his master's at TU Delft in the Netherlands. We compared notes on our experiences so far\u2014both 