icularly focusing on geometric interpretations of various classification boundaries. Elena's background in mathematics allowed her to provide helpful alternative explanations for some of the more abstract concepts. We then discussed approaches to the classification project, agreeing to independently implement baseline models before next week's meeting, where we'll compare results and strategies for improvement. This collaborative learning approach is proving valuable for reinforcing individual understanding while gaining exposure to different perspectives. The afternoon brought a significant academic milestone\u2014my first Machine Learning problem set was returned with an A grade and positive feedback. Dr. Sharma specifically commended my clear mathematical derivations and thoughtful discussion of real-world implications. This validation of my academic capabilities in this challenging course has significantly boosted my confidence. It confirms that despite differences in educational background and culture, I can excel in this international academic environment. After classes, I attended a technical talk organized by the Computing Society featuring a software architect from Google's Dublin office. The presentation focused on scalable system design principles, with case studies from Google's infrastructure. The content complemented our Distributed Systems and Cloud Computing coursework perfectly, providing industry context for academic concepts. During the networking session afterward, I introduced myself to the speaker and asked about internship opportunities. He recommended applying for Google's summer internship program, which opens applications in October, and suggested specific project areas that align with my interests in distributed systems and cloud infrastructure. This kind of direct industry connection represents exactly the professional networking opportunity I hoped for when choosing to study internationally. In the evening, I joined a video call with fri